bly  unrealistic and more of a way for the  author to insert themselves into a world  but they are always the best at  everything possible and admired by all  characters around them and my primary  example of this sort of storytelling is  always the book ready player one where  the protagonist always has the answer to  every problem and everyone around him is  just constantly high-fiving how cool and  resourceful he is demon slayer manages  to take that idea and portray it much  more convincingly by giving tandro a  hell of a lot of emotional weight and  while all those around him do come to  admire respect or at the very least  acknowledge him in some way those  feelings are well and truly earned and  generally not through a single action  either but through a series of actions  that come to endear characters to him in  fact I'd say that tandra has a very  Luffy lack quality about him he's  someone who can  versus world and constantly be capable  of winning people over not by being the  best at anything but by displaying his  unrelenting natural determination so I'm  quite drawn to him and very keen to see  how he progresses going forward the  other side of that coin though is of  course sneza Coe so I said my first  impressions video that I hadn't really  gotten to seen offered to form a proper  opinion and in general I do wish that I  had gotten to see more fur in the series  because every time she was on screen she  was a delight in some way Nezzer Co has  a wonderful mixture of adorable moments  spiced in with the same sort of  admirable determination that  characterizes her brother and that  really is the crux of the series for me  I love the idea of this partnership and  the familial bonds because you almost  never see it in a shown series in fact  family is generally the weakest element  with any parents or siblings if they  aren't dead relegated to the background  but in demons say a family is the  selling point and despite the fact that  most of Tonto his 